                <pb facs="0337" n="289" sameAs="frus:frus1877/pg289"/>

                <head>No. 159.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Read
                        to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1877-04-12"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 12, 1877</date>.
                        (Received May 1.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 259.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that I have
                    succeeded in obtaining a satisfactory copy, in plaster, of a very ancient and
                    remarkable Greek treaty, engraved upon a marble column, one portion of which was
                    found sixty and the other two parts twenty days ago, in the course of the
                    important excavations on the southern slope of the Acropolis which are being
                    carried on by the Archaeological Society of Athens, of which I am an honorary
                    member.</p>
                <p>This is the first inscription which has been discovered relating to the political
                    life of Alcibiades, the brilliant kinsman of Pericles. It is my intention to
                    present the plaster copy to the Department of State, as a pendant to the
                    venerable treaty between the Athenians and the Chalcidians which I had the honor
                    to send to the Department last year.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JOHN MEREDITH READ.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
